{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts fairness based on a faulty mechanistic argument: it states the per-face bias from 2^31 mod 6 = 2 is 'diluted over 10000 draws, keeping counts within ±52.7 of the mean.' This is a non-sequitur. The residue 2^31 mod 6 = 2 means the modulus has 357913941 full cycles plus a remainder of 2 states. Since gcd(6, 2)=2, only half the faces can be slightly over-represented and the other half under-represented, but this does not guarantee the net per-face bias stays under 52.7. In fact, the reasoning inverts the logic: the only actual evidence of fairness available is the simulation tally (which it cites as a countercondition to its own refutation). The claim restates a conclusion ('all six counts fall within two std devs') as a premise and supports it with an unfounded assertion about the bound of LCG modulus bias, contradicting the problem's stated premise that 'there is no closed-form shortcut' to know the counts. A claim that assumes fairness to argue for fairness, while misstating the actual distributional mechanics, fails as a substantive reasoning artifact.","counterexample":null}]}